user prompt:
Regex to delect all <g> elements containing a string `transform="matrix(0.998638,0,0,-0.998638,0.39215,439.799858)"` please. there can be line breaks too.

response:
To create a regular expression (regex) that matches and deletes all `<g>` elements containing the specific `transform` attribute value, including possible line breaks, you can use the following regex:

```regex
<\sg\s[^>]*transform="matrix\(0\.998638,0,0,-0\.998638,0\.39215,439\.799858\)"[^>]*>(.*?)<\/g\s>
```

Here's a breakdown of the regex:

- `<\sg\s`: Matches the opening `<g>` tag with optional spaces.
- `[^>]*`: Matches any characters that are not `>`, including line breaks.
- `transform="matrix\(0\.998638,0,0,-0\.998638,0\.39215,439\.799858\)"`: Matches the `transform` attribute with the specific value, including the `matrix` function and the floating-point numbers. Note that the floating-point numbers are escaped with backslashes to ensure they are matched literally.
- `[^>]*`: Matches any characters that are not `>`, including line breaks.
- `(.*?)`: Matches any characters (non-greedily) in the content of the `<g>` tag.
- `<\/g\s>`: Matches the closing `</g>` tag with optional spaces.

### Example in Python

If you want to use this regex in a Python script to delete such elements from an XML string, you might do something like this:

```python
import re

# Sample XML content
xml_content = '''
<svg>
    <g transform="matrix(0.998638,0,0,-0.998638,0.39215,439.799858)">
        <rect x="10" y="10" width="50" height="50" />
    </g>
    <g transform="matrix(0.998638,0,0,-0.998638,0.39215,439.799858)">
        <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="20" />
    </g>
    <g transform="identity">
        <circle cx="100" cy="100" r="20" />
    </g>
</svg>
'''

# Regex to match and delete the <g> elements
pattern = re.compile(r'<\sg\s[^>]*transform="matrix\(0\.998638,0,0,-0\.998638,0\.39215,439\.799858\)"[^>]*>(.*?)<\/g\s>', re.DOTALL)

# Replace the matched <g> elements with an empty string
cleaned_content = pattern.sub('', xml_content)

print(cleaned_content)
```

This script will remove all `<g>` elements that contain the specified `transform` attribute value. The `re.DOTALL` flag is used to make the dot (`.`) in the regex match newline characters as well.
