user prompt:
how can i use css flexbox to put an image on the same line as a paragraph and have the paragraph automatically format itself around the top and bottom of the picture

response:
To achieve this using CSS Flexbox, you can wrap your image and paragraph inside a flex container. The image will be placed on the same line as the paragraph, and the paragraph will automatically wrap around the image, formatting itself around the top and bottom of the image.

Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. **Create a flex container**: Use a flex container to manage the layout.
2. **Align the image and paragraph**: Use Flexbox properties to align the elements as needed.
3. **Ensure the paragraph wraps around the image**: This is handled by default with Flexbox.

Here's an example:

### HTML
```html
<div class="flex-container">
  <img src="your-image-url.jpg" alt="Your Image Description" class="flex-item img-responsive">
  <p class="flex-item">Your paragraph text goes here. The image will sit next to it, and the text will wrap around the image.</p>
</div>
```

### CSS
```css
.flex-container {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap; /* Allows the paragraph to wrap around the image */
  gap: 10px; /* Optional: Adds space between the image and paragraph */
}

.flex-item {
  flex: 1; /* Ensures the items take up the available space */
}

.img-responsive {
  max-width: 100%;
  height: auto;
}
```

### Explanation:
1. **Flex Container (`flex-container`)**:
   - `display: flex;` makes the container a flex container.
   - `flex-wrap: wrap;` allows the flex items to wrap onto new lines if there isn't enough space.
   - `gap: 10px;` (optional) adds space between the image and the paragraph.

2. **Flex Items (`flex-item`)**:
   - `flex: 1;` makes the image and paragraph take up the available space evenly.

3. **Image (`img-responsive`)**:
   - `max-width: 100%;` ensures the image scales down if it doesn't fit the container.
   - `height: auto;` maintains the aspect ratio of the image.

This setup will place the image and paragraph on the same line, and the text will wrap around the image, automatically positioning itself around the top and bottom of the image.
